description.narrative:
During the project's first year, Diakonia completed the contracting process with the nine implementing partners (IPs), namely:

- Synergie des Missions d'Observation du Cycle Electoral (SYMOCEL);

- Communaute Famille Chretienne (CFC);

- Avocats des Droits de l'Homme (ADH);

- Dynamique pour le Droit, la Democratie et le Developpement Durable (D5);

- Voix des Sans Voix (VSV);

- Initiatives pour le Developpement Integral (IDI);

- African's Witness for Peace and Justice (AWPJ);

- Comite National Femme et Developpement (CONAFED);

- Centre d'Etudes sur Handicap, Justice et Resolution 1325 (CEAHJ1325); 

Overall, the results framework tracking for year show a satisfactory level of achievement. A strengthening of the organisational and operational capacity of the IPs has been noted. On average, 80% of IPs have access to and use governance tools. To ensure the effectiveness of their interventions, IPs have set up 80 change structures, 76 of which are operational in the intervention area and 4 of which will be installed at the beginning of year 2 by the AWPJ partner. These change structures are composed of 951 active members, including 331 women (35%), 322 men (34%), 108 People with disability (PWD) (11%) and 190 young people (20%) engaged in the areas of citizen participation, gender justice and human rights.

To make specific groups able to claim and defend their rights, 1.356 people were informed, sensitized and trained on their fundamental rights in the intervention zones, including 300 Human Rights Defenders (HRDs), 600 members of Local Human Rights Committee (CLDHs), 60 prisoners (N'dolo and Boma prisons) and 396 people (107 women, 155 young people, 35 PWD, 99 men) from 13 communities living in mining zones in Lualaba province.

34 grassroots human rights monitoring structures have been set up by D5, VSV, ADH and AWPJ, and 359 (three hundred and fifty-nine) cases of human rights violations and abuses have been documented and reported, including 168 cases denounced and brought to the attention of the relevant authorities. Change agents involved at grassroots level in the defense of human rights were able to produce 112 monitoring reports, and carried out 111 human rights advocacy actions, of which 21 (19%) were successful and the others are ongoing. 37 meetings and exchanges were organised with the authorities at various levels. Actions to demand and defend rights have been initiated by HRDs, namely 29 press releases, 3 forums for media expression, 1 press conference, 4 warning letters, 4 denunciations of abuses perpetrated by law enforcement officers.  

Initiatives to promote the political participation of women, young people and people with disabilitY were carried out, including one (1) on the representativeness of women in decision-making positions in the Direction of National Education and New Citizenship, where there are only 2 women out of 56 decision-makers (0.03%) in Kasai Central. To this end, 54 women, including 23 women with disability and 16 young people, have pledged to join political parties to position themselves in future elections. In terms of active participation in the electoral process, 4,500 people, 70% of them women, were reached by the CFC partner's awareness campaigns in the 10 group support structures (SEG) in the Lemba, Ngaba and Muanda sites. 3 mass awareness campaigns on electoral participation were conducted in the same sites, reaching at least 30,000 people. Following awareness-raising and mobilisation for the elections, a 2% variation was observed between the 2018 and 2023 legislative elections in the 3 project sites. In addition, 24 Long-Term Observers (LTOs) were deployed in the 6 provinces (Kongo Central, Kasaï Central, Lualaba, Kwilu, Mai Ndombe and Kinshasa) following training on the SYMOAPP data collection tool, which provided real-time observation data and led to the writing of the combined election observation report for December 2023.

In light of the above results, the project's first year went well, with actions carried out in the three areas of intervention: human rights, democracy and gender equality.
